/-!
# Multiplier ideals of pairs and point separation: target signatures

This file is not the roadmap. The definitive document for this folder is `README.md`. This file
states the roadmap's headline theorems against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti, as far as they are
statable there.

The headlines are Nadel vanishing for pairs (9.10c), global generation of adjoint bundles (9.12b),
separation of points for adjoint bundles (the remark of 9.12c) and Kollár's two-point separation
(9.12a). Of these only the separation of points (the remark of 9.12c), on a smooth projective
variety with an ample line bundle, is statable at the pins, and it is stated below as
`adjoint_separates_points`. It needs two
definitions: the intersection numbers `(L^d · Z)` of a line bundle with the closed integral
subschemes of every dimension `d`, which `selfIntersection` computes as the `d`-th difference of
`m ↦ χ(Z, L^m)` (the numerical intersection of the README Conventions, Stacks, Definition 33.45.3
(0BEP)), and the canonical bundle `det Ω_{X/k}` of a smooth variety, `canonicalBundle`. The
statement of 9.12c itself, the two-point form over `ℂ` for a nef and big line bundle, is not a
headline and is not stated here.

The other three headlines are statements about pairs `(X, Δ)` on a normal projective variety over a
field of characteristic zero, and are not statable at the pins:

* Nadel vanishing for pairs (9.10c): if `X` is a normal projective variety over a field of
  characteristic zero, `Δ` an effective `ℚ`-Weil divisor with `K_X + Δ` `ℚ`-Cartier and `L` a
  Cartier divisor with `L − (K_X + Δ)` nef and big, then `H^i(X, O_X(L) ⊗ J(X, Δ)) = 0` for every
  `i > 0`. It lacks the canonical Weil divisor of a normal variety of dimension at least two,
  `ℚ`-Weil divisors with the `ℚ`-Cartier condition, nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisors, log
  resolutions with the pullback of `ℚ`-Cartier divisors and the round-up, and the multiplier ideal
  `J(X, Δ)` of a pair (9.10b), a push-forward from a log resolution. Tau Ceti has Weil divisors
  with integer coefficients (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.SchemeWeilDivisor`), Cartier divisors of an
  integral scheme (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.CartierDivisor`) and the cohomology
  `TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.Cohomology` of a sheaf of modules, so the vanishing
  itself would be expressible once these exist.
* Global generation of adjoint bundles (9.12b): over `ℂ`, if `(X, Δ)` is a projective klt pair
  of dimension `n` with `Δ` effective,
  `N` a nef `ℚ`-Cartier divisor, `M` a line bundle numerically equivalent to `K_X + Δ + N`, `x` a
  closed point and `c_1, …, c_n > 0` with `Σ_d d/c_d ≤ 1` and `(N^d · Z) > c_d^d` for every
  subvariety `Z ∋ x` of positive dimension `d`, then `M` is generated by global sections at `x`.
  It lacks, besides the carriers above, klt pairs (the discrepancies of 9.10a over all normal
  birational models) and numerical equivalence with the `ℚ`-Cartier divisor `K_X + Δ + N`.
* Kollár's two-point separation (9.12a): over `ℂ`, if `(X, Δ)` is a projective klt pair of
  dimension `n ≥ 1` with `Δ` effective, `N` a nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisor, `x₁ ≠ x₂` closed
  points and `c(1), …, c(n) > 0` with `(N^e · Z) > c(e)^e` for every subvariety `Z` of dimension `e`
  through `x₁` or `x₂` (`Z = X` included) and `Σ_e 2^{1/e}·e/c(e) ≤ 1`, then every line bundle `M`
  whose degree on every complete curve is that of `K_X + Δ + N` has a global section vanishing at
  exactly one of `x₁`, `x₂`. It lacks klt pairs, nef and big `ℚ`-Cartier divisors, and the degree
  of `K_X + Δ + N` on curves for a normal `X` (Tau Ceti's `LineBundleClass.intersectionNumber`
  gives the degree of a line bundle on a curve, not of a `ℚ`-Cartier divisor built from the
  canonical divisor).

Nadel vanishing with `Δ = 0` on a smooth variety is Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing, a target of
AlgebraicModuliForArithmeticGeometry §9.10, not of this roadmap. Kodaira's lemma and asymptotic
Riemann–Roch (9.10d), the Bertini theorem for a birational base-point-free linear system, division
by `D` sections with simple common zeros and the bound for sections by intersection numbers with a
generated line bundle (9.13, 9.13a, 9.13b) are statements used with the headlines, not
headlines, and they are not stated here.
-/

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.RiemannHypothesisForDirichletLFunctions.MultiplierIdealsOfPairsAndPointSeparation

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry

universe u

/-- The intersection number `(a^r · X)` of a line-bundle class `a` on a scheme `X` over a field `K`
with `X` itself, as the `r`-th difference
`Σ_{j=0}^{r} (−1)^{r−j} binom(r, j) χ(X, a^j)` of the Euler characteristic, `χ` being Tau Ceti's
`Scheme.Modules.eulerCharBelow` truncated at `r + 1`. For `X` projective over `K` of dimension `r`
the cohomology vanishes above degree `r`, `m ↦ χ(X, a^m)` is a polynomial of degree at most `r`,
and this difference is `r!` times its coefficient of `m^r`: the numerical intersection of the README
Conventions (Stacks, Definition 33.45.3 (0BEP); CyclotomicClassGroupsAndStickelberger §4.3), which
is the degree of `c_1(a)^r ∩ [X]` (DynamicalDegreesOfRationalMaps §2.2). For `r = 1` it is
`χ(a) − χ(O_X)`, Tau Ceti's degree `LineBundleClass.eulerDegree`. -/
noncomputable def selfIntersection (K : Type u) [Field K] (X : Scheme.{u}) [X.Over (Spec (.of K))]
    (r : ℕ) (a : LineBundleClass X) : ℤ :=
  ∑ j ∈ Finset.range (r + 1), (-1 : ℤ) ^ (r - j) * (r.choose j : ℤ) *
    LineBundleClass.lift (fun L ↦ Scheme.Modules.eulerCharBelow K X L.obj (r + 1))
      (fun _ _ ⟨e⟩ ↦ Scheme.Modules.eulerCharBelow_congr K e (r + 1)) (a ^ j)

/-- The canonical bundle `det Ω_{X/k}` of a scheme `X` smooth of relative dimension `n` over a
field `k`: the determinant of the locally free sheaf of relative differentials, of rank `n`. On a
smooth variety it is `O_X(K_X)` (README 9.12c). It is not Tau Ceti's
`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.InvertibleSheaf.canonicalBundle`, a line bundle on a curve built from
the divisor of a Weil differential and not identified there with the Kähler differentials. -/
noncomputable def canonicalBundle (k : Type u) [Field k] (X : Scheme.{u}) [X.Over (Spec (.of k))]
    (n : ℕ) [SmoothOfRelativeDimension n (X ↘ Spec (.of k))] : InvertibleSheaf X :=
  haveI := SmoothOfRelativeDimension.smooth n (X ↘ Spec (.of k))
  (FiniteLocallyFreeSheaf.determinant X n).obj
    ⟨FiniteLocallyFreeSheaf.relativeDifferentials k X,
      fun x ↦ FiniteLocallyFreeSheaf.rank_relativeDifferentials_apply k n x⟩

/-- **Separation of points for adjoint bundles** (Angehrn–Siu 1995, Theorem 0.3; the remark of
README 9.12c). Let `k` be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, `X` a smooth
projective variety over `k` of dimension `n`, `L` an ample line bundle on `X` and `x_1, …, x_ℓ`
distinct closed points of `X`. If
`(L^d · Z) > (½n(n + 2ℓ − 1))^d` for every closed integral subscheme `Z` of `X` of dimension
`d ≥ 1` (`Z = X` included), then for every `i` the adjoint bundle `K_X ⊗ L` has a global section
that is nonzero at `x_i` and vanishes at every `x_j` with `j ≠ i`. As every residue field `k(x_i)`
is `k`, this is the surjectivity of `H⁰(X, K_X ⊗ L) → ⊕_i (K_X ⊗ L) ⊗ k(x_i)`. This statement is
not in the README's [FormalProof], which has only the two-point form over `ℂ` that README 9.12c
states, with the conclusion that some section vanishes at exactly one of the two points; the
passage from `ℂ` to `k` is this roadmap's own, proved by the reduction in the route of the remark of
README 9.12c. -/
theorem adjoint_separates_points (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] [CharZero k]
    (X : Scheme.{u}) [X.Over (Spec (.of k))] [IsIntegral X] [X.IsProjectiveOver k]
    (n : ℕ) [SmoothOfRelativeDimension n (X ↘ Spec (.of k))]
    (L : InvertibleSheaf X) (hL : (LineBundleClass.mk L).IsAmple)
    (ℓ : ℕ) (x : Fin ℓ → X) (hx : Function.Injective x) (hcl : ∀ i, IsClosed ({x i} : Set X))
    (hZ : ∀ (Z : Scheme.{u}) [Z.Over (Spec (.of k))] [IsIntegral Z] (ι : Z ⟶ X)
      [IsClosedImmersion ι] [ι.IsOver (Spec (.of k))] (d : ℕ), 1 ≤ d →
      topologicalKrullDim Z = d →
      ((n * (n + 2 * ℓ - 1) : ℚ) / 2) ^ d <
        selfIntersection k Z d (LineBundleClass.pullback ι (LineBundleClass.mk L))) :
    ∀ i, ∃ s : Γ((InvertibleSheaf.tensorProduct (canonicalBundle k X n) L).obj, ⊤),
      x i ∈ (InvertibleSheaf.tensorProduct (canonicalBundle k X n) L).obj.nonvanishingLocus s ∧
      ∀ j, j ≠ i →
        x j ∉ (InvertibleSheaf.tensorProduct (canonicalBundle k X n) L).obj.nonvanishingLocus s := by
  sorry

end TauCetiRoadmap.RiemannHypothesisForDirichletLFunctions.MultiplierIdealsOfPairsAndPointSeparation
